.TH LIBZZ9K 3 "1 November 2026" "libzz9k"
.SH NAME
libzz9k, zz9k_inflate, zz9k_pool \- ZZ9000-family board services, with a software fallback
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <zz9k/amix.h>
.PP
.BI "int zz9k_inflate(uint32_t " algorithm ", uint32_t " flags ,
.BI "    const void *" src ", uint32_t " src_length ,
.BI "    void *" dst ", uint32_t " dst_capacity ", int " how ,
.BI "    ZZ9KDecompressResult *" result ", int *" ran );
.PP
.BI "int zz9k_pool(const ZZ9KAmixBoard **" boards );
.BI "int zz9k_pool_codec(uint32_t " flags );
.BI "void zz9k_pool_timeout(uint32_t " ms );
.B "void zz9k_pool_close(void);"
.PP
.BI "int zz9k_open_unit(ZZ9KContext **" ctx ", int " unit );
.fi
.PP
.B cc ... -I/opt/amix/include ... -Wl,--gc-sections -L/opt/amix/lib -lzz9k -lz
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Z3660, and a ZZ9000 whose firmware has the services runtime, can do work for a
program on the board's second ARM core.
The kernel reaches each such board through
.BR zz9ksvc (7),
.B /dev/zz9ksvc0
and
.BR /dev/zz9ksvc1 .
.B libzz9k
is the program's side: the ZZ9000 SDK version 2 host interface
.RB ( <zz9k/host.h> )
over those channels, and calls that run on a board when one offers the service
and in software when none does, with the same result either way.
.PP
.B zz9k_inflate
decompresses
.I src_length
bytes at
.I src
into at most
.I dst_capacity
bytes at
.IR dst .
.I algorithm
is
.BR ZZ9K_COMPRESSION_DEFLATE_RAW ,
.B ZZ9K_COMPRESSION_ZLIB
or
.BR ZZ9K_COMPRESSION_GZIP ;
the other algorithms of the SDK run on a board only.
.I flags
is 0 or
.B ZZ9K_DECOMPRESS_FLAG_EXPECT_END
(a stream that does not end is an error).
.I how
is
.B ZZ9K_INFLATE_AUTO
(a board that offers it, else software),
.B ZZ9K_INFLATE_BOARD
or
.BR ZZ9K_INFLATE_SOFT .
The status is a
.B ZZ9K_STATUS_*
code;
.B ZZ9K_STATUS_OK
fills
.I result
(bytes consumed and written, the CRC-32 of the output, the algorithm, the result
flags), and the status, the result and the bytes at
.I dst
are the same on a board and in software.
.B ZZ9K_STATUS_NO_MEMORY
means
.I dst
is too small,
.B ZZ9K_STATUS_BAD_REQUEST
a stream that cannot be decoded or a bad argument.
.I *ran
(when
.I ran
is not null) says where it ran.
With
.B ZZ9K_INFLATE_BOARD
and no board offering it, the status is
.B ZZ9K_STATUS_NOT_FOUND
when there is no board and
.B ZZ9K_STATUS_UNSUPPORTED
when none has the service.
.PP
The pool opens every
.B /dev/zz9ksvcN
once per process, at the first call, and reads each board's capability
catalogue (QUERY_CAPS, and the CODEC service's flags by QUERY_SERVICE).
A node that gives
.B ENXIO
(no board, or no runtime on it) is left out.
.B zz9k_pool
gives the boards and their number;
.B zz9k_pool_codec
the unit of the first board whose CODEC service has every flag in
.IR flags ,
or \-1.
.B zz9k_pool_timeout
bounds each board request
.RI ( ms ,
0 for the kernel's 10 seconds);
a call that times out runs in software, and that board is left out of
.B ZZ9K_INFLATE_AUTO
calls for 30 seconds.
.B zz9k_pool_close
closes every channel.
.PP
.B zz9k_open
takes the first unit that answers,
.B zz9k_open_unit
a given one, for the SDK's own calls.
On Amix a buffer of
.B zz9k_alloc_shared
is mapped with
.BR mmap (2);
the kernel passes on only the requests it knows (see
.BR zz9ksvc (7));
.BR zz9k_submit ,
.B zz9k_poll
and the interrupt calls give
.BR ZZ9K_STATUS_UNSUPPORTED .
.SH NOTES
The library has a section per function:
.B -Wl,--gc-sections
keeps only the calls a program makes (the whole SDK host interface is about 50 KB
of text).
.PP
The kernel gives a freed buffer that was mapped back to the board only when the
channel closes, so the pool keeps two buffers per board and reuses them, and
opens its channel again when growing them runs out of handles.
.PP
The library is not safe for threads.
.SH LICENCE
.B libzz9k
is GPL-3.0-or-later: it is the ZZ9000 SDK's host code, which is GPL-3.0-or-later
with no linking exception.
A program that links it is a combined work under the GPL, version 3 or later, as
a whole.
The licence is
.BR /opt/amix/doc/libzz9k/LICENSE .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /opt/amix/lib/libzz9k.a
the library
.TP
.B /opt/amix/include/zz9k
its headers
.TP
.BR /dev/zz9ksvc0 ", " /dev/zz9ksvc1
the channels
.SH SEE ALSO
